{"toolCallId":"call_4f2fac1d5d62417f81e600bf37b2f174","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n<!-- FILL: Write three sentences: the problem, the change and the proof. GOOD: Callers parse a 17-line report to read one value. The flag prints one line. A line count proves it. WEAK: This document describes the feature. -->\n\n[2-3 sentence high-level overview for stakeholders who need quick context]\n\n**Key Decisions**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Major decision 1], [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Major decision 2]\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Blocking dependency]\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n<!-- FILL: Fill every row: level, priority, status, date and branch. Use the real branch name. GOOD: Priority P1. Status Draft. Branch `feat/recommend-level-quiet`. WEAK: Priority TBD. Status maybe. -->\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: P0/P1/P2] |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n<!-- FILL: State the problem in two sentences: what breaks, and for whom. GOOD: The recommend-level script prints a 17-line report, so a caller that needs only the level cannot parse the answer. WEAK: The script has issues with output. -->\n\n### Problem Statement\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n<!-- FILL: List three to five things this change does, each starting with a verb. GOOD: Add a `--quiet` flag that prints only the recommended-level line. WEAK: Update the script and docs. -->\n\n### In Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 1]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 2]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 1] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 2] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: path/to/file.js] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Modify/Create/Delete] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Write one testable requirement per row. Name the exact command or output. GOOD: With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line that starts with `Recommended Level:`. WEAK: The flag should work well. -->\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-002 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n<!-- FILL: Write one measurable outcome per line. A command can prove it. GOOD: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15` prints one line and exits 0. WEAK: The feature is successful. -->\n\n- **SC-001**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: Name one dependency and one risk. Give each a concrete mitigation. GOOD: A caller parses the level line and breaks when the label changes. Mitigation: keep the label stable and test it. WEAK: There may be some risk. -->\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: System/API] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What if blocked] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: High/Med/Low] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Name one measurable limit per line. Write `N/A` and one reason when the packet runs no service, or delete the whole section. GOOD: The flag adds no measurable time: the same 40 ms run. WEAK: Performance is good. -->\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Response time target - e.g., <200ms p95]\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth requirement - e.g., JWT tokens required]\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Uptime target - e.g., 99.9%]\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n<!-- FILL: Name the empty input, the maximum input and the failure path. One line each. GOOD: Empty input: the script exits 2 with usage. Maximum: 10,000 files scores in under one second. WEAK: Handle edge cases. -->\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How system handles]\n- Maximum length: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Limit and behavior]\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback behavior]\n- Network timeout: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Retry strategy]\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Recovery behavior]\n- Session expiry: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: User experience]\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n<!-- FILL: Score each row with the number the change earns. The total must match the level. GOOD: Scope 4/25: one file and 12 lines. Total 18/100. WEAK: Complexity is medium. -->\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Files: X, LOC: Y, Systems: Z] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth: Y/N, API: Y/N, Breaking: Y/N] |\n| Research | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /20] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Investigation needs] |\n| Multi-Agent | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Workstreams: X] |\n| Coordination | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Dependencies: X] |\n| **Total** | **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /100]** | **Level 3** |\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n<!-- FILL: List each risk with impact, likelihood and one mitigation. GOOD: R1: a caller parses the label. Impact M, likelihood L. Mitigation: a test pins the label. WEAK: Some risks exist. -->\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Strategy] |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n<!-- FILL: Write one story per user. Name the actor, the want and the benefit. GOOD: As a build script, I want one parseable line, so that I can skip the report. WEAK: Users want a quiet mode. -->\n\n### US-001: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n<!-- FILL: Write each open question as one line. Write `None` when nothing blocks. GOOD: Does `--quiet` suppress warnings? No, stderr is unchanged. WEAK: Edge cases are unclear. -->\n\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Leave the four links in place. -->\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `decision-record.md`\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh prints a 17-line report even when a caller needs only the level. A --quiet flag prints that one line, and every existing invocation keeps its current output.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\nA caller that needs the recommended level from `recommend-level.sh` currently receives a 17-line report and must extract one value from it. This specification adds a `--quiet` flag that prints only the `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` line; every invocation without the flag keeps its current output byte for byte. A line count of one for `--quiet`, 17 for the default report, and the unchanged JSON document prove the change.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` wins over `--json` when both are passed (ADR-001); the quiet line reuses the exact label `output_text()` already prints (ADR-002)\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: none outside the Bash script and the existing Node test harness\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring` |\n| **Estimated LOC** | ~15 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n`recommend-level.sh` prints a 17-line report even when the caller only needs the recommended level. A build script, skill wrapper, or agent that runs the helper to pick a documentation level must pipe the output through `sed` or `grep` to recover one value, and that parser breaks whenever the report's wording changes. The script offers a JSON mode, but a JSON document still needs a parser and carries eleven fields where the caller wanted one.\n\n### Purpose\nPrint exactly the recommended-level line when `--quiet` is passed, so a caller reads one stable string instead of parsing a report, while leaving the default text and JSON output untouched.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Add a `--quiet` flag to the argument parser in `recommend-level.sh` and record it in the script header and `--help`\n- Add a dedicated quiet renderer that prints exactly one line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`\n- Dispatch to that renderer after scoring, before the JSON and text branches\n- Keep the default text report, the JSON document, the exit codes, and stderr unchanged\n- Cover the flag with a case in the existing Node test harness\n\n### Out of Scope\n- A short `-q` alias - the request names `--quiet`, and a second spelling is new surface with no caller\n- Any change to scoring, thresholds, phase recommendation, or confidence - the flag changes presentation only\n- Suppressing stderr - errors must stay visible so a quiet run cannot be mistaken for a valid level\n- Updating the skill README or the level-decision matrix - neither documents individual options\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add the flag, the quiet renderer, the dispatch branch, and the help text |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-validation.js` | Modify | Add one case that asserts the one-line contract |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | The argument parser accepts `--quiet` and sets a `QUIET` flag without consuming a value. |\n| REQ-002 | With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line matching `^Recommended Level: [0-3] \\((Quick\\|Baseline\\|Verification\\|Full)\\)$`. |\n| REQ-003 | Without `--quiet`, the default text report is unchanged: the Level 3 fixture still prints 17 lines. |\n| REQ-004 | `--quiet` takes precedence over `--json`; the JSON document is not printed when both are passed. |\n| REQ-005 | `--quiet` does not change exit codes: 0 on a valid run, 1 on invalid input. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-006 | The script header OPTIONS block and `show_help()` both document `--quiet`. |\n| REQ-007 | stderr is unchanged: invalid input still prints its error to stderr under `--quiet`. |\n| REQ-008 | The quiet line is produced by a dedicated output function, so all three renderers read the same scored state. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural` prints exactly `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)` and exits 0.\n- **SC-002**: The same invocation without `--quiet` prints 17 lines, the count observed before the change.\n- **SC-003**: `--json` without `--quiet` still emits a document that `JSON.parse` accepts and that names `recommended_level`.\n- **SC-004**: `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-validation.js` reports one more passing case than its 51-case baseline and no failures.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | Bash 3.2 or newer, already required by the script | The flag cannot ship if the interpreter floor moves | No new shell feature is used; `bash -n` stays the syntax gate |\n| Dependency | Node test harness in `runtime/cli/tests/` | No regression coverage for the new branch | Add one case to the existing file rather than a new runner |\n| Risk | The quiet label drifts from the text report | Callers that parse either form break | One shared string, pinned by AC-002 and AC-003 |\n| Risk | `--quiet --json` surprises a JSON consumer | A parser receives text | Precedence is documented in help and ADR-001, pinned by AC-004 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: `--quiet` adds no measurable work; it prints one line where the default report prints 17, so the run stays within the same tens of milliseconds.\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: The flag reads no environment variable, file, or credential; it selects a renderer and nothing else.\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: `set -euo pipefail` stays in force and `bash -n` reports no syntax error on the edited script.\n- **NFR-R02**: Every path that exits 1 today still exits 1 with `--quiet`, and every path that exits 0 still exits 0.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--quiet` with neither `--loc` nor `--files` exits 1 with the existing stderr error and prints no stdout line.\n- Maximum input: `--quiet --loc 1000000 --files 1000` prints the same single line, naming Level 3.\n- Repeated flag: `--quiet --quiet` is accepted and still prints one line.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- Non-numeric value: `--quiet --loc abc` exits 1 with `ERROR: --loc must be a positive integer` on stderr and an empty stdout.\n- Unknown option: `--quiet --bogus` exits 1 with `ERROR: Unknown option: --bogus`.\n- Conflicting output mode: `--quiet --json` prints the text line and no JSON, per ADR-001.\n\n### State Transitions\n- `--no-recommend-phases` with `--quiet`: the quiet line is unaffected because the phase section never reaches stdout.\n- `--help` with `--quiet`: help is printed and the script exits 0; quiet does not suppress help regardless of argument order.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 6/25 | Files: 2, LOC: ~15, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 3/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N |\n| Research | 4/20 | Read one 780-line script and two test files |\n| Multi-Agent | 1/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 0 external |\n| **Total** | **16/100** | **Level 3** |\n\nThe score describes the change, not the packet: 16/100 is Level 1 territory by the helper's own mapping. This packet carries Level 3 because it was scaffolded at that level, and the architecture sections record decisions a later reader would otherwise have to rediscover from the diff.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | A caller parses `Recommended Level:` and the label changes | M | L | Keep the exact label; AC-002 pins the regex |\n| R-002 | `--quiet` accidentally suppresses stderr errors | H | L | Quiet changes only the success renderer; AC-006 pins exit 1 with a silent stdout |\n| R-003 | `--quiet --json` returns text to a JSON consumer | M | M | Documented precedence in help and ADR-001; AC-004 pins it |\n| R-004 | The quiet line drifts from the text report | L | L | One shared string built from the same globals; AC-002 compares both |\n| R-005 | The 17-line default report changes as a side effect | M | L | AC-003 pins the line count |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: One parseable line (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** build script or skill wrapper, **I want** the recommended level on a single line, **so that** I can read it without parsing a report.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:**\n1. Given a task that scores 70 or more, When I run `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural`, Then stdout is exactly `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)` and the exit code is 0.\n2. Given a task that scores below 25, When I run `--quiet --loc 10 --files 1`, Then stdout is one line naming Level 0 and nothing else.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Existing callers keep their output (Priority: P0)\n\n**As an** existing caller, **I want** the default text and JSON output unchanged, **so that** my current parser keeps working.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:**\n1. Given no `--quiet`, When I run the Level 3 fixture, Then stdout is the same 17 lines it printed before the change.\n2. Given `--json` without `--quiet`, When I run the Level 3 fixture, Then stdout parses as JSON and names `recommended_level`.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: A quiet run still reports failure (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** script author, **I want** errors to stay on stderr, **so that** a quiet run cannot mistake a failure for a level.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:**\n1. Given invalid input, When I run `--quiet --loc abc`, Then stdout is empty, stderr holds the error, and the exit code is 1.\n2. Given `--help`, When I run `--quiet --help`, Then the help text is printed and the exit code is 0.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Should `--quiet` also suppress the phase recommendation fields? **RESOLVED: yes by construction** - the quiet renderer prints one line and never calls the phase section.\n- Should the flag have a `-q` short form? **RESOLVED: no** - the request names `--quiet`, and a second spelling is new surface with no caller.\n- Should `--quiet --json` be an error instead of a precedence rule? **RESOLVED: precedence**, recorded in ADR-001, because a caller that appends `--quiet` to an existing command line should still receive a level.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `plan.md` (L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD)\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":90,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":63}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
